WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.900
Whether they were offensive,&nbsp;
sickening, or just plain boring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:07.920
these science fiction movies had viewers&nbsp;
walking out long before the credits rolled.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.480
Despite being a billion-dollar franchise, the&nbsp;
"Transformers" films have never been without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.700
their throngs of critics who roundly reject them&nbsp;
as poorly written excuses to splash big-budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.740
effects all over the screen. Horror legend Stephen&nbsp;
King once famously remarked on Twitter that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.420
first "Transformers" was the only movie he'd ever&nbsp;
walked out on. But it's the second "Transformers"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.200
movie, "Revenge of the Fallen," that seems&nbsp;
to have drawn most people's ire online.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:36.000
On Reddit, "Revenge of the Fallen" regularly&nbsp;
pops up in topics where users ask what movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.320
people walked out on. With a thin story,&nbsp;
several extremely annoying characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:45.534
and an excessive runtime of two and a half&nbsp;
hours, it's no surprise viewers wanted out early.

00:00:51.935 --> 00:00:53.534
"Now that's rude."

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:54.840
But before you jump to blaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.320
director Michael Bay, know that he&nbsp;
might not have been entirely at fault.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.540
Just before production on the film was set to kick&nbsp;
off, the Writer's Guild of America went on strike,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:08.280
forcing Bay and his team into a crunch to&nbsp;
deliver a script. According to Bay himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.720
they had just three weeks to turn in a story.&nbsp;
And with that, the whole thing makes a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.740
sense. Although even star Shia LaBeouf&nbsp;
criticized the film for lacking heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.120
it still wound up with nearly $900&nbsp;
million in box office receipts.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.720
Today, the 1968 sci-fi epic "2001: A Space&nbsp;
Odyssey" is seen as one of the most groundbreaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.060
films in the genre. Directed by the legendary&nbsp;
Stanley Kubrick, its impact on science fiction is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.280
immeasurable, with countless cinephiles hailing it&nbsp;
as a breathtaking achievement in cinema. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.120
there are just as many people, both then&nbsp;
and now, who hate the movie for its slow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.680
plodding, and often dialogue-free story.&nbsp;
Audiences have been baffled by the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.480
for decades — and when it was initially released,&nbsp;
that befuddlement led many to outright reject it.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.680
If reports are to be believed, the&nbsp;
premiere of "2001" in New York saw not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.660
just individual walk-outs — not even&nbsp;
dozens — but nearly 250 disgruntled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.100
patrons exit the screening. According to The&nbsp;
Hollywood Reporter, a separate screening in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:09.780
Los Angeles led to a similar exodus, with&nbsp;
star actor Rock Hudson allegedly asking,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.468
"What is this bulls—?"

00:02:11.468 --> 00:02:16.140
Reviews of the film were decidedly mixed, with&nbsp;
some lauding the film for its scope, scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.940
and jaw-dropping visual storytelling. However,&nbsp;
just as many people heaped hate on the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.720
calling it a slow drag that went&nbsp;
nowhere and achieved nothing.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.540
Writer, actor, and director David Cronenberg&nbsp;
is no stranger to films so grotesque that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.680
repulse movie-goers. As the master of body&nbsp;
horror, that's kind of his bread and butter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.820
From his earliest films like "The Brood" and&nbsp;
"Videodrome" to the mainstream hit "The Fly,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.920
Cronenberg has always reveled in his trademark&nbsp;
nausea-inducing visuals. Given his track record,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:49.140
a wave of walk-outs for his 2022 film "Crimes&nbsp;
of the Future" could have easily been predicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:54.000
by anyone. And it was … by Cronenberg&nbsp;
himself. The filmmaker told Deadline,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.980
"There are some very strong scenes. I mean, I'm&nbsp;
sure that we will have walkouts within the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.900
five minutes of the movie. I'm sure of that. Some&nbsp;
people who have seen the film have said that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:08.160
think the last 20 minutes will be very hard on&nbsp;
people and that there'll be a lot of walkouts."

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.840
"I am making the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.200
first for myself, and then for an audience.&nbsp;
I don't really know what that audience is."

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:22.740
The film, starring Viggo Mortenson, Lea Seydoux,&nbsp;
and Kristen Stewart, is a sci-fi body horror flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:27.960
in which humans undergo painful malformations&nbsp;
to adapt to their new artificial environments.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.640
Sure enough, there were plenty of audience&nbsp;
members who left the theater in disgust — at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.440
according to The New York Times' Kyle Buchanan&nbsp;
on Twitter. It's certainly not the first time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.700
the director, whose explosive movie "Scanners'&nbsp;
was said to have prompted similar walk-outs.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.360
Remember how Stephen King said that&nbsp;
the only movie he ever walked out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.380
on was "Transformers?" That means he must not&nbsp;
have seen the sequel to one of his own films:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:54.060
"The Lawnmower Man 2: Beyond Cyberspace."&nbsp;
We can say that with confidence, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:59.520
as King himself hated the first movie so much&nbsp;
he sued the studio to have his name removed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.800
the credits. The sequel was even worse, taking a&nbsp;
simple thriller and turning it into mind-numbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.180
schlock — and sure enough, many audience&nbsp;
members didn't feel compelled to stick around.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:14.160
As a cheaply made, quickly slapped-together&nbsp;
follow-up, "Lawnmower Man 2: Beyond Cyberspace"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:19.620
is an overly complex, poorly acted film with&nbsp;
embarrassing special effects. Needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:24.180
it really isn't worth finishing. Critic&nbsp;
Stacy Richter for the Tucson Weekly knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.020
something was wrong right from the get-go,&nbsp;
as the screening she attended had just four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.520
other patrons present. Little did she&nbsp;
know just how bad it would be. She ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.180
up doing the unthinkable: she walked,&nbsp;
something forbidden for a movie critic.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.420
Richter wasn't the only one;&nbsp;
anecdotes can be found all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.080
over the web of horrified viewers who&nbsp;
switched the sequel off before it ended.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.360
Released in 2008, the J.J. Abrams-produced&nbsp;
"Cloverfield" landed in cinemas with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.400
great fanfare. The film was promoted with a&nbsp;
groundbreaking viral marketing campaign, though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.900
was a classic monster movie in every sense, just&nbsp;
with a modern twist. Filmed in a handheld style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.420
"Cloverfield" wowed audiences with its story of a&nbsp;
world-ending monster that rises from the ocean to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.040
destroy New York City. Unfortunately, not all&nbsp;
audiences could make it all the way through.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.240
This time, however, it wasn't because&nbsp;
it was bad, boring, or even confusing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.980
There was actual science behind it: some&nbsp;
moviegoers were affected by the jittery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:21.480
shaky nature of the found footage film,&nbsp;
and many walked out due to motion sickness.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:26.040
"Cloverfield" is far from the only movie&nbsp;
to prompt such walk-outs. Neill Blomkamp's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:30.360
"District 9" was said to have induced a few&nbsp;
similar reactions, but they were a drop in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.220
bucket compared to the widespread problems seen&nbsp;
during screenings of "Cloverfield." ABC News,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.240
CNN, and other outlets all covered&nbsp;
audience members' bouts of nausea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.540
prompting WebMD to publish a&nbsp;
report about the phenomenon.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.000
"I don't feel so good."

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:49.560
A decade before the "Cloverfield"&nbsp;
monster reached the shores of New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.880
Roland Emmerich and Dean Devlin tried&nbsp;
to do the same thing with "Godzilla" by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:58.920
bringing the iconic Japanese kaiju across the&nbsp;
pond. Reviews were unkind, to say the least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:05.220
as critics lambasted 1998's "Godzilla" as a&nbsp;
dumbed-down, over-the-top FX spectacle with no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:10.980
soul and far too much bluster. Actor Kenpachiro&nbsp;
Satsuma, who wore the Godzilla suit in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:16.620
1980s-era Toho films, said he walked out of the&nbsp;
American remake — and many others did the same.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:21.600
All over forums online, you'll find diehard&nbsp;
fans of "Godzilla" who claim to have exited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.760
the theater mid-movie. Criticisms range from&nbsp;
jabs at Godzilla's iguana-like redesign to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:31.740
complaints about the poor acting and plodding&nbsp;
story. One Reddit user put it nicely, saying,

00:06:31.740 --> 00:06:34.440
"Incredibly boring, boring enough that even though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.200
I was a poor kid I was happy&nbsp;
to lose the money I spent."

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.060
For some, "Godzilla" '98 changed how they&nbsp;
approached new releases. Another unhappy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:46.140
ticket-holder on Reddit claimed they'd never&nbsp;
go to see a movie on opening night ever again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:49.980
after "Godzilla." This user pledged to&nbsp;
instead wait for reviews to roll in.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.440
Regarded as one of the worst movies of all time,&nbsp;
"Battlefield Earth" is based on a science fiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.600
novel by L. Ron Hubbard. Unfortunately, the film&nbsp;
version was also a passion project for Hubbard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.340
Church of Scientology, and it has gained a&nbsp;
reputation as "the Scientology movie." Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:10.860
the film is borderline incoherent, with haphazard&nbsp;
editing and directing throughout. Meanwhile, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:15.360
performances from its all-star cast — including&nbsp;
John Travolta, Forest Whitaker, and Kelly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.400
Preston — are so ham-fisted and cringe-worthy,&nbsp;
it quickly becomes an unintentional comedy.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:26.400
Essentially, "Battlefield Earth" is a big-budget B&nbsp;
movie, so it's no wonder audiences felt hoodwinked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.440
and wanted out before it was over. Viewers refer&nbsp;
to its goofy plot points and comical attempts at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:36.000
serious science fiction storytelling when asked&nbsp;
what forced them to leave the theater. Legendary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.620
critic Roger Ebert put it best in his review of&nbsp;
the film, which begins with the bold statement:

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:43.980
"'Battlefield Earth' is like taking a&nbsp;
bus trip with someone who has needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:48.480
a bath for a long time. It's not merely&nbsp;
bad; it's unpleasant in a hostile way."

00:07:49.055 --> 00:07:51.860
"Hahahahahaha!"

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:53.580
"Stupid humans."

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.860
There are some movies that people walk out&nbsp;
of that seem surprising in hindsight. A few,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:04.140
like "2001: A Space Odyssey," become all-time&nbsp;
classics. Others seem promising when audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:09.600
file into the theater, only to disappoint like&nbsp;
1998's "Godzilla." Then there are those movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.340
that look so bad even in the trailers that one&nbsp;
has to question why people purchased tickets in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:19.560
the first place. One example is "Battleship"&nbsp;
— a sci-fi action flick based on the iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:23.640
family board game that prompted more than&nbsp;
one movie-goer to get going before it ended.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:27.180
Sure, the producers of "Battleship" could&nbsp;
have simply gone for a straightforward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.740
naval war film. Instead, the filmmakers went&nbsp;
as literal as possible by turning the little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:36.060
plastic gaming pegs into the devastating&nbsp;
weapons of an invading armada of aliens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:40.620
That didn't quite add up for audience members,&nbsp;
many of which kicked it to the curb and left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.760
screenings early. Though the effects are&nbsp;
top-notch and the action isn't terribly bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.780
the silly characters and nonsensical&nbsp;
story are what really sank "Battleship."

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.340
While some movies are abandoned midway&nbsp;
because they're boring, confusing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:57.660
offensive, or just downright stupid, you'd&nbsp;
be hard-pressed to find one that's all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:01.560
those at the same time and more. But there's&nbsp;
at least one franchise film that landed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.420
a ton of hype and a fair amount of promise that&nbsp;
sadly devolved into pathetic schlock, sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:11.220
audiences to the exits before it was over — and&nbsp;
that would be the 2018 clunker "The Predator."

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.060
Written and directed by Shane Black,&nbsp;
who had worked on the 1987 original,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:18.780
there was plenty to be excited about&nbsp;
when "The Predator" was first announced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:22.380
Skeptical viewers probably should have&nbsp;
stuck to their gut on this one, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:28.680
as the movie is a total disaster from start to&nbsp;
finish. Among the film's offenses: a sloppy story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:33.540
with poor action sequences, misguided attempts&nbsp;
at humor that left fans feeling betrayed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:38.160
and an autistic character whose role in the&nbsp;
story offended many viewers. Over on Reddit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:42.000
some were particularly irked by the film's&nbsp;
use of autism as a major plot point.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.400
On Bloody-Disgusting.com, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.900
fans seemed more upset by the movie's&nbsp;
overly edgy comedy. One user commented,

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:52.380
"Easily the worst in the series.&nbsp;
I walked out of the cinema when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.140
the Predator used a detached arm&nbsp;
to give someone the thumbs up."

00:09:55.860 --> 00:09:58.980
As the most expensive movie ever&nbsp;
made at the time of its release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.980 --> 00:10:04.680
Kevin Costner's "Waterworld" is seen today as one&nbsp;
of the biggest box office blunders in history. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.420
highly anticipated sci-fi actioner, it stars&nbsp;
Costner as a post-apocalyptic hero in a grim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:14.700
future world where water covers the Earth and dry&nbsp;
land has become all but a myth. A sort of reverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:18.960
"Mad Max," "Waterworld" had the potential&nbsp;
to be the next big post-apocalyptic classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.220
but it wound up sinking in theaters&nbsp;
instead. It received scathing reviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:27.660
for its dismally uninteresting screenplay&nbsp;
and wooden performances. And according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.840
director Kevin Reynolds, it was also&nbsp;
the victim of the dreaded walk-out.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.920
Speaking to Newsweek in 2015,&nbsp;
Reynolds discussed the poor early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:39.780
reception and the uphill battle he faced&nbsp;
— especially from movie critics. He said,

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.220
"The press wanted it to bomb. The head of&nbsp;
Universal, Bill Washington, thought the bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:50.100
publicity cost us at least $50 mill in the box&nbsp;
office [...] The reviews were painful. People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.100 --> 00:10:54.480
didn't like it. I remember publicists saying&nbsp;
that people walked out of a press screening."

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:59.100
It wasn't just the critics who left early;&nbsp;
"Waterworld" regularly makes people's lists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.720
of movies they simply couldn't finish. Today,&nbsp;
opinions on "Waterworld" have softened somewhat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:07.680
but it's still an unfortunate blemish&nbsp;
on Costner's otherwise sterling resume.

